From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Awe Morris <awe@noctvm.io>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 09:10:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 18/19] x86/pc9800: do not reserve the PC/AT I/O map

The generic x86 resource table describes PC/AT devices.  On PC-9800 it
claims ports belonging to unrelated hardware; notably the AT timer range
0x40-0x43 overlaps the PC-9800 keyboard at 0x41/0x43.  This prevents native
drivers from reserving their devices and also mislabels the resource map.

Skip the AT-only reservations for the compile-time PC-9800 target.  Native
PC-9800 drivers reserve the ports they own.

Signed-off-by: Awe Morris <awe@noctvm.io>
---
 arch/x86/kernel/setup.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c b/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
index f1fea506e..6bd52380e 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/setup.c
@@ -518,6 +518,16 @@ void __init reserve_standard_io_resources(void)
 {
 	int i;
 
+	/*
+	 * This table describes the PC/AT I/O map.  Several ranges are active
+	 * PC-9800 devices instead: 0x41/0x43 is the keyboard and 0xf0 is the
+	 * reset port, for example.  Reserving the AT ranges prevents the native
+	 * PC-9800 drivers from claiming their hardware.  Leave ownership to the
+	 * native drivers rather than installing knowingly false reservations.
+	 */
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_X86_PC9800))
+		return;
+
 	/* request I/O space for devices used on all i[345]86 PCs */
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(standard_io_resources); i++)
 		request_resource(&ioport_resource, &standard_io_resources[i]);
